<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Further Pomerania</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Farther Pomerania, Hinterpommern</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Further <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a> is the eastern part of historical Pomerania, lying east of the Oder along the southern Baltic coast, with towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slupsk">Stolp</a> (Słupsk), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koszalin">Köslin</a> and Stargard. It was ruled by branches of the Griffin dukes, passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg-prussia">Brandenburg-Prussia</a> in 1653 and became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> after 1945.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/further-pomerania.jpg" alt="Further Pomerania" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Andrzej Otrębski · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kolobrzeg_latarnia_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
